 "why_comparable": "Both scores are the share of that window's valid cells exceeding the 99th percentile of the same sensor's own ordinary change. Under the null both are 1% by construction, so the two are on a common footing even though raw \u0394z distributions differ between sensors. Raw \u0394z must never be mixed; a placebo-normalised exceedance rate can be.",
 "null_check": {
  "optical": 0.00623364394129468,
  "radar": 0.010001046316964286
 },
 "threshold_asymmetry": {
  "optical_p99": 0.3169780373573303,
  "optical_construction": "pooled over three placebo pairs (M82)",
  "radar_p99": 0.24988050758838654,
  "radar_construction": "one placebo pair of its own orbit-19 contract, time-matched",
  "disclosure": "the two thresholds are built from a different number of placebo pairs; this is an asymmetry in the fusion, not a controlled variable",
  "bias_direction": "The null check quantifies which way the asymmetry leans: optical placebo cells exceed their threshold 0.62% of the time against radar's 1.00%. The optical threshold is therefore slightly stricter, so optical windows are mildly disadvantaged in the fused ranking and radar windows mildly favoured. This matters because radar takes 1st, 2nd and 6th place: some of that lead is contract, not signal. Equalising the two nulls is an open correction."
 },
